  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nRegional radar mosaic indicates a band of showers along a\nconfluence zone dissipating as it tracks east near I-55 early\nthis afternoon. An outflow boundary well ahead of this activity\nwas tracking toward SE IL, and appears to be the focus for\nisolated thunderstorm development this afternoon. Thermodynamic\nanalysis indicates MLCAPE values building to around 1000-1500 \nJ/kg across southern portions of the forecast area, primarily \nnear and south of Interstate 70. Weak 06 km bulk shear around \n20-25 knots will tend to limit storm organization, but steep \nlow- level lapse rates driving DCAPE near 1000 J/kg will lead to\npulsey downburst winds in any thunderstorms that manage to \ndevelop mid- late afternoon. This activity should quickly fade \nearly evening with diminishing instability.\n\nMoisture transport ramps up again overnight, ahead of a \nshortwave diving southeast from the Plains. Precipitable water \n(PWAT) values will climb to 1.82.0 inches along an elevated \nboundary stretched west-east over central IL, well above the \n90th percentile for early September. Hi-res model guidance \ndepict clustered convective development tracking eastward \nlate tonight and Thursday morning, mainly south of the I-72 \ncorridor. Probabilities from the HREF reveal a 70 percent chance\nof rainfall exceeding 1.0 inch south of the I-70 corridor, with\nlocalized maxima up to 2.0 inches across southeast Illinois by \nThursday morning. Pavement ponding and localized nuisance urban \nflooding will be the primary impacts.\n\nShowers are expected to dissipate over the south half of the\narea Thursday morning. Light winds will prevail as the frontal\nzone slowly sinks south, leading to another day of moderate\nhumidity levels, especially south of I-72. Dry and mild weather\nwill dominate Friday as high pressure builds through the \neastern Great Lakes.\n\nEnsemble guidance generally agrees on the next front reaching\ncentral IL this weekend. Upper level support doesn't look great\nand we appear to be near the tail end of the front, so we \nmaintain low chance PoPs for Saturday and Saturday night. \nTemperatures will average a bit above normal Sat-Sun with highs \nin the mid- upper 80s. A building upper-level ridge across the \ncentral CONUS early next week will foster a further warming \ntrend for Monday and Tuesday, pushing high temperatures into the\nlower 90s. Furthermore, the Climate Prediction Center (CPC) \n6-10 day outlook indicates a 40 to 70 percent probability of \nabove-normal temperatures across central Illinois through \nSeptember 18th.",
